  'published' => '<h1>Who made this thing?</h1><div><div>I did. My name is Roberto Perez-Franco. I was born in Panama in 1976. I started writing Symbulator (called at the time Symbolic Circuit Simulator) on April 1999. Back then I was a junior in a dual Mechanical and Electrical engineering degree at Panama\'s <a href="http://utp.ac.pa/">UTP</a>. By early 2001, Symbulator\'s fifth version (nicknamed \'Q\') was out, and served as my graduation thesis. Symbulator has brought me a lot of joy. It got me a Distinguished Alum award from UTP in 2008, and was at least part of the reason for the Outstanding Young Person award that the Panamanian Chapter of the Junior Chamber International gave me in 2010. Although I have no evidence for it, it may have also played a part in my getting in 2002 the Fulbright scholarship to come to MIT for a Masters in 2003.</div><div><br></div><div>I killed the last known bug of Symbulator Q on April 2003. During the following decade, with Symbulator a mature product, I focused my attention in completing my Masters, a PhD and postdoc at MIT. Currently I work at MIT as a Research Associate (<a href="http://mit.edu/roberto">mit.edu/roberto</a>). Halfway through 2013, I noticed Symbulator was still being used around the world, even though I had put no work in it for ten years. This moved me to start working on a comprehensive documentation of Symbulator, in English, and - while working on the documentation - to improve Symbulator. Soon I was making so many changes that I decided a new release was warranted. Thus Symbulator 6 was born.<br></div></div><div><br></div><div>When I\'m not working at MIT or coding Symbulator, I like to <a href="http://roberto.perez-franco.com/">write</a> and <a href="http://zirie.com/">paint</a>, sometimes under the moniker \'Zirie\'. I am married since 2000 to the best wife I could ever wish for, and I have a small daughter. Although we are all Panamanians, for the sake of my commute to MIT, we currently live in Massachusetts.</div>
',
